            <ArticleTitle>Interstitial cystitis: the painful bladder syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>137-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Interstitial cystitis (IC) is an enigmatic and frustrating condition to manage as a physician and to cope with as a patient. Many patients are declared as drug addicted and neurotic. Social status and number of sexual partners showed no correlation with incidence. Patients report moderate to excruciating pain in the suprapubic or vaginal area, urgency and frequency (up to 50 micturitions/d), and disruption of their social life. Diagnosis of IC is made by exclusion. Different pathophysiologic mechanisms have been proposed: changes in epithelial permeability, pelvic floor dysfunction, mastocytosis, activation of c-fibers, and increase of nerve growth factors and bradykinin. No single theory can explain IC.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
